The deeper Pantheon principle

I’d condense the whole botanical lesson into:

Wound-Guided Development

\boxed{
\text{wound}
\rightarrow
\text{signal}
\rightarrow
\text{environment inference}
\rightarrow
\text{resource reallocation}
\rightarrow
\text{architectural change}
\rightarrow
\text{new phenotype}
}

And recursively:

\text{new phenotype}
\rightarrow
\text{new exposure}
\rightarrow
\text{new wounds}
\rightarrow\cdots

The important shift is that Pantheon stops asking only:

How do I survive damage?

and instead asks:

What does the shape of this damage tell me about the world, and what should I become because of it?

That’s almost a biological version of the Delta Observatory.

And there’s an elegant connection to the “second book” you just described: a reader reconstructing Pantheon from its artifacts could look not only at what Pantheon contains, but at its scars. Missing branches, redundant concepts, unusual protections, repeated metaphors and architectural detours become evidence about the pressures under which the system evolved.

The scar becomes another page in the probability book.